---
/**
 * ATHLETIC MANAGEMENT — slice R6.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Athletic Management":
 *
 *   Entrance      D — THE SPREAD. The photograph breaks the LEFT page
 *                 trim before any type. Everyday Legends directly
 *                 above opens on a display-scale mark hung in columns
 *                 1-2 with no photograph at all; this opens on a
 *                 frame that runs off the sheet. That is the turn.
 *   Column window frame 1-7 off the left trim · detail 4-7 ·
 *                 lede 8-12 · body 8-12 · closing 8-11 · caption 1-3
 *   Image         `bleed-left` — mcclain-bleachers.jpg
 *                 PLUS `inset` — athletics-detail.jpg, lapped upward
 *   Rhythm        open. 110px, matching Educational Leader, which is
 *                 the other of the page's two tallest sections.
 *   Margin holds  the caption — and it is on the LEFT.
 *   Length        FULL — opening statement + two paragraphs + coda.
 *
 * THIS IS THE PAGE'S SINGLE SPREAD MOMENT AND ITS ONLY BROKEN GUTTER.
 * CONTEXT.md's image-tier table allots exactly one `bleed-left` and
 * exactly one `inset` on the whole page, and both are spent here. It
 * is also the only section carrying two photographs; CONTEXT.md's
 * "photography does not sit in adjacent sections" rule names this
 * pair as the deliberate exception.
 *
 * THE SECTION MIRRORS THE PAGE, INCLUDING ITS MARGIN. Every section
 * from About down runs type left and marginalia right, on a margin
 * edge that has held at x=1066 since About. Athletic inverts it, and
 * because text at 8-12 leaves no room on the right, the caption hangs
 * at columns 1-3 on the sheet's LEFT trim. CONTEXT.md's R6 note is
 * explicit that this breaks the right-margin continuity on purpose
 * and that the break is what makes the inversion legible. It is not
 * to be "restored".
 *
 * NO SECTION NUMERAL. The table's original margin cell read "numeral
 * + caption"; CONTEXT.md's R6 note supersedes it with "caption only".
 * No shipped section carries a numeral, and a lone numeral on a page
 * whose Index runs 01-05 in a different order is the exact confusion
 * the decorative-marks rule exists to prevent — flagged four times.
 *
 * COPY: SET VERBATIM FROM COPY.md, "Athletic Management". Nothing is
 * reworded, nothing is added, nothing is restored from the Appendix.
 *
 * ONE LINE IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. "Every decision is viewed through
 * a larger lens: Legacy" belongs to the Feature Quote one screen
 * below and appears on this page EXACTLY ONCE. COPY.md marks it as a
 * deletion for placement, not a rewrite. Do not restore it here; a
 * second instance dilutes the page's one structural pivot.
 *
 * THE CAPTION TEXT IS NEW COPY AND IS NOT IN COPY.md. COPY.md's
 * "never add copy without sign-off" rule applies to anything added
 * after the September 4 rewrite, so this line REQUIRES JACKSON'S
 * APPROVAL BEFORE LAUNCH. It is built, not substituted.
 *
 * OSU CLEARANCE IS UNRESOLVED AND THIS SECTION RESPECTS THE INTERIM
 * RULE IN FULL. No school name is rendered anywhere in this section,
 * in markup or in a comment — the copy says "Division I" and the
 * caption says "Division I", which are nominative and generic. No
 * logo, no mark, no team photography, no school-keyed colour. The
 * helmet in the bleachers frame is separately cleared by Jackson,
 * September 4 2026, with no crop restriction. AND: Jaylen's 2026 OSU
 * captaincy has not been publicly announced by the client and is not
 * surfaced here in any form.
 *
 * NO grid-column IS SET IN THIS FILE. Five collapse-aware primitives
 * carry every placement — .win-ath-bleed, -text, -close, -caption,
 * -detail — and each one's behaviour in all four bands (>=1280 /
 * 1024-1279 / 751-1023 / <=750) is written out in full above the
 * implementation in global.css, before the implementation.
 *
 * ONE grid-row IS SET, on .win-ath-bleed, and it is STRUCTURAL rather
 * than a measurement: the type column is authored in exactly four
 * blocks — head, lede, body, closing line — so the frame spans from
 * the first to the last. It is not a line count and it does not go
 * stale when the copy rewraps. The CAPTION and the DETAIL are seated
 * in the row below it by DOCUMENT ORDER alone, with no row number and
 * no offset.
 *
 * ONE TYPE LEFT EDGE, NOT THREE. CONTEXT.md's staircase is a device
 * for type anchored to the sheet's own left trim. This section's type
 * is anchored to a photograph, so under the rule R4.1 added, its left
 * edge is a function of the frame's right trim plus one gap — column
 * 8 follows column 7 and is not chosen. That is permitted only
 * because there is NO EMPTY COLUMN between the frame and the type,
 * which is verified by measured x in every band. The lede, the body
 * and the closing line therefore differentiate on the RIGHT edge
 * instead, which is R3's lede 4-11 / closing 4-9 relationship
 * transposed onto this window.
 *
 * NO TRANSFORM ON EITHER PHOTOGRAPH, EVER. Both crops are done by
 * object-position, and the detail's additionally by oversizing the
 * <img> box inside overflow:hidden. A static transform: scale() on an
 * <img> silently kills the mix-blend-mode treatment — measured in R1,
 * the treated backdrop reverted to the raw source colour — because
 * the transformed image composites into its own layer.
 *
 * NO BORDER, NO SHADOW, NO ROUNDED CORNERS on either frame.
 *
 * GOLD: ZERO NEW. Both budgets are spent — 3 saturated type accents
 * (the Legacy tagline, About's drop cap, the pull quote) and 8
 * hairline-gold rule contexts. No type in this section is gold and
 * this section contains NO RULE AT ALL, so it adds 0 of the 8. The
 * two frames carry the page's existing photograph treatment, which
 * multiplies by Gold; that is reuse of a shipped treatment layer and
 * is neither of the two things the budget counts. THE CAPTION IS
 * SMOKED SLATE, per CONTEXT.md's explicit "no gold rule" on captions.
 *
 * OVERLAP: ONE, AND IT WAS ALREADY BUDGETED. CONTEXT.md counts the
 * Athletic detail crop as one of the two SPENT overlaps, alongside
 * the Cover name overrunning its portrait. R6 implements that one; it
 * does not spend the third, which stays reserved for Jackson. Nothing
 * else in this section overlaps anything.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import bleachers from '../../brand_assets/mcclain-bleachers.jpg';
import athleticsDetail from '../assets/athletics-detail.jpg';
---

<section class="sheet ath" id="sec-athletics">
  <!--
    SOURCE ORDER IS STRUCTURAL. frame -> head -> lede -> body ->
    closing -> caption -> detail is what makes grid's sparse cursor
    put the caption and the detail in ONE row below the frame: the
    cursor sits past column 11 at the end of the type column, so the
    caption's column 1 is behind it and drops to a new row, leaving
    the cursor at column 4 exactly where the detail asks to start.
    Reordering these siblings changes the composition. See global.css.

    THE CAPTION IS AUTHORED BEFORE THE DETAIL AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
    MECHANISM. Authored after it, the cursor would sit past column 7
    and the caption's column 1 would be behind it again — a second row
    drop, putting the caption UNDER the detail instead of beside it,
    and needing a hardcoded grid-row to get it back. Document order
    makes the placement cancel instead, which is what CONTEXT.md asks
    for and what R3, R4 and R5 all already do.

    It is also a defensible stacked reading order below 750:
    photograph, name, subtitle, opening statement, the two paragraphs,
    the closing line, the caption, then the detail. The caption
    reading as an introduction to the frame below it is ordinary
    editorial behaviour — captions sit above photographs as often as
    below them — and the B5 ruling makes it the SECTION's one caption
    rather than either photograph's.
  -->
  <figure class="win-ath-bleed ath-bleed" aria-labelledby="ath-caption">
    <!--
      THE BLEED FRAME. `bleed-left` tier — the only one on the page.

      IT RUNS OFF THE LEFT PAGE TRIM, past --gutter, to the viewport
      edge, and that MUST be unambiguous. CONTEXT.md's own overlap
      floor applies to a bleed for the same reason it applies to an
      overlap: a frame that stops a few pixels short of the trim, or
      crosses it by a few, reads as a misaligned image rather than as
      a broken gutter. The negative margin below is exactly --gutter,
      so the frame's left edge is the viewport's left edge at every
      width in every band, with nothing to sweep.

      IT IS NOT CROPPED WIDE AT ANY BAND. The source is presented
      1984x2976 — see the EXIF note on .ath-frame — and the frame is
      0.95, which is still portrait. CONTEXT.md refuses a horizontal
      band at spec time rather than solving it by cropping a vertical
      photograph, and that refusal is upheld here including at <=750,
      where the frame is full-bleed to both trims: a tall portrait at
      full viewport width is not a wide crop.

      NO BORDER, NO SHADOW, NO ROUNDED CORNERS.

      Eager + sync decode: a photograph that resolves late is absent
      from full-page captures and from any PDF the client generates.
    -->
    <div class="plate-img ath-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX. This frame's box IS the
        frame — the crop is vertical only, so there is no oversized
        box here and the box width is the frame width. Measured at the
        WIDER end of each band, as a fraction of the viewport:

          <=1145   full bleed, both trims           1145 -> 100vw
          <=1279   64 + 6t + 5g, t=(vw-392)/12      1279 ->  50vw
          else     64 + 7t + 6g, t=(vw-392)/12      1440 ->  57vw

        The break is at Athletic's own 1145 crossover, not at a shell
        band boundary, because that is where the frame's WIDTH changes
        — the shell's 1023 and 750 thresholds do not move it, since it
        is already full-bleed at both.
      -->
      <Image
        src={bleachers}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain seated on stadium bleachers in a cream suit, a football in one hand and a helmet on the bench beside her."
        widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600, 1984]}
        sizes="(max-width: 1145px) 100vw, (max-width: 1279px) 50vw, 57vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
    </div>
  </figure>

  <div class="win-ath-text ath-head">
    <!--
      THE <h2> IS THE SECTION NAME. CONTEXT.md's locked heading
      structure: each section's <h2> is the element whose text names
      the section, and that string equals the folio label. Verified
      against src/data/sections.ts — { id: 'sec-athletics', n: '06',
      label: 'Athletic Management' } — not assumed.

      The head sits at the top of the type block on the type block's
      own left edge, in the established .role register. Everyday
      Legends directly above uses the same register, and that is
      correct: CONTEXT.md makes the SUBTITLE a shared device across
      the pillars and the ENTRANCE the thing that must differ. R5
      opens on a display-scale mark hung outside its type; this opens
      on a photograph that leaves the page.
    -->
    <h2 class="role ath-name">Athletic Management</h2>
    <!-- Set verbatim from COPY.md, middle dots and all. Sentence case
         against the uppercase name above it, so the head has two
         registers rather than two identical-looking lines. -->
    <p class="ath-subtitle">Momager &middot; Athletic Manager to the McClain Brothers</p>
  </div>

  <p class="win-ath-text ath-lede">Long before NIL, there were early mornings, long drives, and decisions no one else saw.</p>

  <div class="win-ath-text ath-body">
    <p>As the mother of three football players moving through the high school and Division I levels, Dr. McClain&rsquo;s role grew organically from motherhood into strategy.</p>
    <p>Her approach is intentionally bigger than the game. Opportunities and contracts matter, but so do character, financial literacy, reputation, and preparation for life after football.</p>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE CLOSING LINE. It steps in ONE COLUMN ON THE RIGHT and keeps
    the body's left edge — the only differentiation available to a
    section that runs a single type edge. Educational Leader's coda
    does the same thing in mirror image, stepping back OUT to the
    lede's left edge; the two sections use the same relationship on
    opposite edges, which is the difference between a system and a
    repeat.

    NO RULE ABOVE IT. Educational Leader owns the coda-with-a-lid
    entrance and repeating it here would make two of the page's three
    coda-bearing sections open their codas identically. The gap does
    the work instead — see .ath-body's derived bottom margin.
  -->
  <p class="win-ath-close ath-close">The goal was never simply to develop successful athletes. It is to raise young men who understand what their name is worth.</p>

  <!--
    THE CAPTION — the section's one, belonging to the bleed frame.
    Hung at columns 1-3 on the sheet's left trim, below the frame,
    beside the detail.

    ⚠️ THIS TEXT IS NEW COPY AND IS NOT IN COPY.md. It requires
    Jackson's approval before launch. Four words, factual, editorial,
    far inside CONTEXT.md's eight-word ceiling, and NEVER file
    metadata. It names no school, so it raises no OSU clearance
    question, and it does not surface Jaylen's unannounced 2026
    captaincy.

    R6.1 REPLACED THE PREVIOUS LINE AND THE REASON IS WORTH KEEPING.
    R6 shipped "Three sons, high school through Division I" — 42
    characters against a window that holds 27 at the caption register,
    which forced a container-derived font-size rendering 8.93px at
    1440 and 6.82px at 1146. The sizing was the correct response to
    the copy it was given; the copy was the problem.

    Montserrat, small, letterspaced, uppercase, Smoked Slate at
    reduced opacity, via the page's existing .plate-caption primitive
    — reused rather than reinvented, so this section's caption and any
    future one sit in the same register. NO GOLD: CONTEXT.md's caption
    rule says so outright and the budget is spent regardless.

    It is the <figure>'s accessible name via aria-labelledby rather
    than a nested <figcaption>, because a <figcaption> would have to
    live inside the frame's box — which bleeds off the trim and ends
    at column 7 — and could not then hold columns 1-3 of the sheet.
    The association survives; only the nesting does not.
  -->
  <p class="win-ath-caption plate-caption ath-caption" id="ath-caption"><span class="ath-caption-line">Three sons, three programs</span></p>

  <figure class="win-ath-detail ath-detail">
    <!--
      THE DETAIL CROP. `inset` tier — the only one on the page, and
      already counted by CONTEXT.md as one of the two SPENT overlaps.

      IT LAPS UPWARD ONTO THE BLEED FRAME BY A DERIVED AMOUNT, NOT A
      PIXEL. See --ath-lap below. Grid placement plus a negative
      margin, never position:absolute with a hard offset — the overlap
      has to survive a resize, and an absolutely positioned inset
      would need a new offset at every width.

      WHAT IT COVERS, AND WHAT IT MUST NOT. It sits at the frame's
      bottom-right and covers a strip of bleachers and her lower legs.
      It does NOT cover her face, which the crop puts at 21-31% of the
      frame's height, and it does NOT cover the football, which the
      crop puts at 61-77% of the frame's height and left of the
      detail's own left edge. Both verified from the render, not
      asserted.

      THE TWO FRAMES SHARE A RIGHT EDGE, expressed as a shared grid
      line so it cannot drift: the frame ends on line 8 and the detail
      ends on line 8, line 7 in band 2, line 5 in band 3. That shared
      edge is what makes the pair read as interlocking rather than as
      two photographs that happen to be near each other.

      A DIFFERENT CROP FROM EVERY OTHER FRAME ON THE PAGE. 0.70
      against the bleed frame's 0.95 — CONTEXT.md: aspect ratio is a
      separate lever from width, and no two photographs on the page
      share a crop.

      NO CAPTION OF ITS OWN. The B5 ruling: one caption for the
      section. The detail is the same subject matter in the same
      frame and a second caption would be file metadata with a line
      break in it.
    -->
    <div class="plate-img ath-detail-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX, WHICH IS 138.9% OF THE
        DETAIL, because that is how the horizontal crop is done
        without a transform. Measured at the WIDER end of each band:

          <=750    62% of 1 col,  x1.389    750 -> 77vw
          <=900    62% of 6 of 8, x1.389    900 -> 59vw
          <=1023   62% of 5 of 8, x1.389   1023 -> 49vw
          <=1145   62% of 7 of 12,x1.389   1145 -> 44vw
          <=1279   3t + 2g of 12, x1.389   1279 -> 30vw
          else     4t + 3g of 12, x1.389   1440 -> 41vw

        The 1279 step is the smallest box on the page and the 1145
        step is nearly half again as large, because the detail stops
        being a 3-column inset and becomes 62% of a stacked measure at
        that boundary. Both were computed at both ends of their band.
      -->
      <Image
        src={athleticsDetail}
        alt="A gold heel resting on a football on a turf field, goalposts out of focus behind."
        widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 750px) 77vw, (max-width: 900px) 59vw, (max-width: 1023px) 49vw, (max-width: 1145px) 44vw, (max-width: 1279px) 30vw, 41vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
    </div>
  </figure>
</section>

<style>
  /* CONTEXT.md's per-section padding table gives Athletic Management
     110 — `open` rhythm, and the same number as Educational Leader,
     which is the other of the page's two tallest sections. Everyday
     Legends directly above is 70, so the step into this section is
     the largest on the page so far and the section announces itself
     before a word of it is read.

     FLAT AT EVERY BAND, which is the pattern R3 set and R4 and R5
     followed: one declaration, no media query. Inventing a scale here
     would put R6 on a rule the three sections above it do not use.

     THE ROW TRACK LIST. Five rows. Rows 1-4 are the type column's
     four authored blocks; row 5 is the photographic footer — caption
     at the left trim, detail at the frame's right trim.

     ROW 4 IS THE ONLY FLEXIBLE TRACK, and that is the same mechanism
     R4.2 uses one section above, for the mirror-image reason. The
     bleed frame spans rows 1-4 and is TALLER than the type column at
     every width that carries the spread; without a flexible track,
     grid would distribute that surplus across all four rows and
     silently inflate the internal type gaps, which are derived from
     the type tokens and MUST NOT be added to. With row 4 flexible,
     grid sizes the frame in the step that distributes space only to
     flexible tracks, so the whole surplus lands in one place — below
     the closing line, where it is ordinary section space, and where
     it also guarantees that row 5 starts exactly on the frame's
     bottom edge. That guarantee is what the detail's negative margin
     is measured from.

     R4.2 needed a flexible track to stop a MARGIN inflating a row;
     R6 needs one to stop a PHOTOGRAPH inflating four. Same tool, and
     in both cases the alternative was a hardcoded height. */
  .ath{
    padding-block: 110px;
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto 1fr auto;
  }

  /* ---- THE BLEED FRAME — entrance D ------------------------------ */

  /* OFF THE LEFT PAGE TRIM. The frame is a grid item at columns 1-7,
     so its box starts at the gutter; a negative inline-start margin
     of exactly --gutter takes it to the viewport edge. It is written
     against the token rather than as 64px, so it follows the gutter
     through all four bands — 64px in bands 1 and 2, 40px in band 3,
     and the clamp in band 4 — with nothing to re-measure.

     `body` already carries overflow-x: hidden, so this cannot open a
     horizontal scrollbar even transiently.

     The RIGHT trim is untouched: it stays on column 7's grid line,
     which is what the type's left edge is measured from. */
  .ath-bleed{
    margin: 0;
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--gutter) * -1);
    /* The frame inside is taken out of flow (below), so the figure
       needs to establish the containing block for it. */
    position: relative;
  }

  /* THE SHAPE FLOOR IS A PERCENTAGE-PADDING SPACER, NOT
     `aspect-ratio`, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION WITH TWO MEASUREMENTS
     BEHIND IT.

     What is wanted is "at least 0.95, and taller if the type column
     is taller" — a MINIMUM shape, not a fixed one, because the
     frame's bottom edge has to be row 5's top edge or the detail's
     lap is spent on empty ground. `aspect-ratio` cannot express a
     minimum, and both ways of trying it failed differently:

       aspect-ratio + height:100%   the ratio computed the WIDTH from
                                    the definite height — 915.42 x
                                    0.95 = 869.64px inside an 819.33px
                                    figure — and the frame covered the
                                    first 26px of every line of type
                                    beside it. When a box has a ratio
                                    and exactly one axis definite, the
                                    ratio computes the other, and
                                    `width: auto` on a stretched grid
                                    item is not definite enough to win
                                    that argument.
       aspect-ratio + height:auto   the width was right and the
                                    stretch never fired: Chrome sizes
                                    the box from the ratio, so 819.33
                                    x 862.44 inside a 915.42px row
                                    span, and the lap fell back to
                                    69.41px.

     `width: 100%` would fix the first and break the bleed, because
     100% of the grid area is the column span and the negative margin
     then pulls the RIGHT trim left by a whole gutter. Auto width is
     what makes the box fill the area PLUS its negative margin.

     A percentage padding-top resolves against the element's own
     WIDTH and only ever contributes a minimum in-flow height, so it
     cannot compute a width from anything and cannot stop the stretch.
     105.263% is 1 / 0.95. The frame itself is taken out of flow and
     pinned to the figure's box, so the spacer governs nothing but the
     floor. */
  .ath-bleed::before{
    content: "";
    display: block;
    padding-top: 105.263%;   /* 1 / 0.95 */
  }

  .ath-frame{
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* THE CROP.

     EXIF FIRST, BEFORE ANY CROP DECISION. mcclain-bleachers.jpg is
     stored 2976x1984 and carries EXIF orientation 6, so every
     renderer presents it as 1984x2976 — a portrait 0.6667. `sips`
     reports the raw buffer and is WRONG on this file; verified with
     sharp().metadata(), which returns width 2976, height 1984,
     orientation 6. Every number below is against the PRESENTED
     1984x2976, not the buffer.

     THE MECHANISM IS OBJECT-POSITION, NOT A TRANSFORM AND NOT AN
     OVERSIZED BOX. This frame is cropped vertically only — the full
     source width is used — so the box IS the frame and no negative
     left offset is needed. At 0.95 the box is wider than the file's
     native 0.6667, so object-fit: cover spreads the full source width
     across it and crops the height; the visible fraction is
     0.6667 / 0.95 = 0.7018 of the source height, and object-position
     Y places that window:

         window top = Y x (1 - 0.7018) = Y x 0.29825

     0.10 of the source height is where the window is wanted, so
     Y = 0.10 / 0.29825 = 33.5%, giving the window [0.100, 0.802].
     The whole expression is in percentages of the source, so there is
     no breakpoint and nothing to sweep. The HORIZONTAL window is
     identical at every viewport width in every band — it is the full
     source width. The VERTICAL window tracks the frame's rendered
     aspect; see the note on .ath-frame below.

     WHAT THAT WINDOW HOLDS, measured off the source rather than
     eyeballed. Source fractions, then where they land in the frame:

         face        y 0.248-0.314   ->  frame y 0.21-0.31
         helmet      y 0.405-0.510   ->  frame y 0.43-0.58
         football    y 0.524-0.638   ->  frame y 0.60-0.77
         her figure  y 0.224-0.862   ->  crosses the bottom trim

     It cuts sky off the top, which is the part of this frame that
     fights the palette hardest, and it cuts her at mid-calf on the
     bottom trim — so the figure terminates against the page edge
     rather than sitting inside the rectangle, and the detail crop
     below then shows the heel the frame just cut. That continuation
     is why the bottom trim is where it is.

     0.95 IS A FLOOR ON THE SHAPE, NOT A FIXED SHAPE, and that is
     deliberate. The figure is a stretched grid item spanning rows
     1-4, so `height: 100%` fills whatever those rows resolve to; the
     aspect-ratio is what the frame contributes to sizing them. So the
     frame is 0.95 whenever it is the taller column and grows to match
     the type column whenever the type is taller. Row 5's top is then
     the frame's bottom edge in every band, which is what the detail's
     lap is measured from — see .ath-detail.

     THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED. With `align-self:
     start` and a fixed 0.95, the type column ran 915.42px at 1440
     against a frame of 862.44px, so row 5 began 52.98px below the
     frame and the declared 122.4px lap rendered as 69.41px of ACTUAL
     overlap — nearer the Cover's failed 54px than its own declared
     depth. Fixing that by choosing a taller aspect would be choosing
     a number that holds at one width and drifts at every other;
     stretching makes it hold everywhere with nothing swept.

     THE FLOOR IS WHAT KEEPS IT PORTRAIT. A purely derived height
     could go landscape wherever the type column is shorter than the
     frame is wide, and CONTEXT.md refuses a wide crop of a vertical
     photograph outright. With the floor the frame can only ever be
     TALLER than 0.95, never wider, at any viewport.

     THE VERTICAL WINDOW THEREFORE MOVES WITH THE FRAME'S HEIGHT; the
     horizontal window does not, because it is the full source width
     at every width in every band. The measured windows per band are
     in the R6 report. Every one of them holds her figure and cuts her
     at mid-calf on the bottom trim, which is the property the window
     is chosen for.

     0.95, AND EVERY SHAPE ABOVE IT, IS PORTRAIT AND UNIQUE ON THE
     PAGE. CONTEXT.md: no two photographs share a crop, and all client
     photography is vertical — no wide crop anywhere, at any band. */
  /* The frame is pinned to the figure by .ath-frame above; the crop
     numbers are all that is left here. */

  /* :global() — <Image> emits its own <img>, which never receives
     Astro's data-astro-cid-*, so a scoped selector silently stops
     matching. This has bitten this project twice. */
  .ath-frame :global(img){
    object-position: 50% 33.5%;
  }

  /* ---- THE TREATMENT --------------------------------------------
     PULLED DOWN HARDER THAN THE STUDIO FRAMES, and CONTEXT.md is
     explicit about why: this is the one candid frame in a set of
     composed ones, and its saturated sky-blue and turf-green fight
     Antique Gold and Cashmere in a way the studio backdrops do not.
     The studio frames may sit closer to natural; this one may not.

     The page's shared treatment is a FLAT Gold multiply at 0.24 —
     flat rather than a ramp because a gradient's corner renders as
     its first stop whatever the stop positions, which put a visible
     light strip in the Cover's frame for two passes. That reasoning
     is unchanged and this frame inherits it; only the opacity moves.

     BOTH FRAMES IN THIS SECTION TAKE THE SAME OPACITY. They are one
     shoot, one subject and one register, and two different multiply
     strengths on two photographs that touch each other would read as
     a colour mismatch at the seam.

     THE VALUE IS SET BY THE EDGE STEP, MEASURED. CONTEXT.md's target
     where a photograph meets the page ground is 35-50 luminance units
     against Porcelain at ~227. The measured numbers and the sweep
     that produced this opacity are in the R6 report; the constraint
     runs in the opposite direction from the Cover's, because this
     frame is LIGHT — bleachers and sky — rather than a mid-grey
     studio backdrop, so its untreated step is too SMALL and the
     multiply has to open it rather than close it.

     MEASURED WARMTH IS NOT PERCEIVED WARMTH: CONTEXT.md records that
     multiplying by Cashmere moves R-B about 4 units, below the
     threshold at which anyone sees a hue change. Gold is the only
     saturated warm in the locked palette and is what this multiplies
     by. The R-B shift and what it actually looks like are both in the
     report, because the numbers alone have been wrong twice. */
  .ath-frame::before,
  .ath-detail-frame::before{
    opacity: 0.44;
  }

  /* ---- THE HEAD -------------------------------------------------- */

  .ath-head{
    /* HEAD -> LEDE. One body line, via the body's own tokens rather
       than a pixel: calc(1.0 x 17px x 1.8) = 30.6px. It re-resolves
       from the type at every width with no breakpoint. R4's number
       and R4's reasoning: a fixed pixel gap set from one width is a
       swept band by another name. */
    margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .ath-name{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: -0.07em;   /* half of .role's 0.14em tracking */
  }

  /* The deck, in the register Educational Leader, Premier Leadership
     and Everyday Legends all share. Set verbatim from COPY.md. */
  .ath-subtitle{
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.012em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 1 of 3, on the shared --lede-size token. Same face, same
     four axes, same line-height and letterfit as About's, Educational
     Leader's, Premier's and Everyday Legends' — CONTEXT.md's lede
     rule. This section differs from its neighbours in ENTRANCE,
     WINDOW and RHYTHM, which is where the difference belongs;
     re-scaling the lede would break the page's one display tier into
     two that mean nothing.

     NO max-width. Measure is controlled by the column span and by
     nothing else. */
  .ath-lede{
    /* LEDE -> BODY. 0.75 of the lede's OWN line-height via `lh`, so
       it re-resolves from the type rather than from a width. R3
       precedent, same number, same unit. */
    margin: 0 0 0.75lh;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);
    line-height: var(--lede-lh);
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3. --bio-size / --bio-lh, the page's body tokens,
     unchanged. NO max-width — span only. Two paragraphs, per COPY.md;
     this section runs FULL because it has no other home. */
  .ath-body{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
    /* BODY -> CLOSING LINE. Two body lines = 61.2px, from the body's
       own tokens. It is the LARGEST internal gap in the section —
       twice the head->lede gap and larger than the lede->body gap —
       so the closing line reads as leaving the text block rather than
       as a third paragraph. Educational Leader marks the same
       relationship with a hairline rule; this section has no rule, by
       design, so the gap carries it alone and has to be bigger. */
    margin-bottom: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .ath-body p{ margin: 0; }
  .ath-body p + p{ margin-top: 1.5em; }

  /* ---- THE CLOSING LINE ------------------------------------------
     Tier 2 of 3, the same coda register About and Educational Leader
     use: Fraunces at opsz 96 / SOFT 35 / wght 400, all four axes
     stated so none is left to Fraunces' defaults of wght 900 / WONK 1
     / SOFT 100. 1.86x the body and 0.58x the lede at 1440 — clearly a
     third tier, subordinate to the lede and distinct from the body. */
  .ath-close{
    /* THE LAST BASELINE SITS ON THE DETAIL PHOTOGRAPH'S BOTTOM EDGE.
       (R6.1.)

       `align-self: end` on the primitive puts this element's MARGIN
       BOX bottom on the bottom edge of its two-row span, which is the
       detail's bottom edge. The last baseline sits above that box
       bottom by the coda's own descender space, so the box is lifted
       by exactly that much — a NEGATIVE margin-bottom, which pushes
       the box down past the row edge until the baseline lands on it.

       Distance from a box bottom up to the last baseline is
       `line-height - B`, where B is the first-baseline offset inside
       a line box:

           B  = lh/2 + (ascent - descent)/2 x font-size

       so  lh - B = 0.5lh - 0.36029em

       using Fraunces' measured ascent - descent of 0.72058em — R2's
       number, measured from rendered ink, reused rather than
       re-derived, because this is the same face at the same coda
       tier that About's closing line uses.

       Hence margin-bottom = -(0.5lh - 0.36029em) = 0.36029em - 0.5lh,
       which is -9.27px at 1440's 32px/1.3 and re-resolves at every
       width from the element's own units. NOTHING HERE COUNTS A LINE
       and nothing is a pixel measured at one width: the body's
       rendered height cancels out of the arithmetic entirely because
       the anchor is the row's END, which is R2's lesson and the third
       time this project has applied it.

       WHY AN IMAGE EDGE AND NOT A TEXT BASELINE. About aligns its
       closing line to body paragraph 2's last baseline, so both terms
       in its offset are line-height-minus-baseline and the expression
       is a difference of two descender spaces. Here the target is a
       photograph's trim, which has no baseline of its own, so only
       this element's term survives and the expression is simpler by
       one half. Same device, one fewer term.

       The R6 build put this line at the top of row 4 with roughly
       900px of Porcelain under it at 1440. It now closes the section
       on one level line with the detail, which is the same thing
       About does at the top of the page. */
    align-self: end;
    margin: 0 0 calc(0.36029em - 0.5lh);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- THE CAPTION -----------------------------------------------
     .plate-caption carries the whole register — Montserrat 10.5px,
     0.22em tracking, uppercase, Smoked Slate at 0.82, set on the
     --baseline line-height with a --baseline top margin. It is the
     page's caption primitive and this is the page's first caption, so
     it is used rather than re-declared.

     0.82 rather than a lighter knock-down is measured, not chosen:
     Slate on Porcelain is 7.54:1 at full strength, 4.45:1 at 0.78 —
     under the floor — and 4.90:1 at 0.82.

     IT SNAPS TO A BODY BASELINE, NOT TO THE FRAME'S BOTTOM. The
     primitive's margin-top is one --baseline unit (28px) and its
     line-height is the same unit, so its baseline lands on the page's
     28px lattice rather than flush against the photograph above it.
     CONTEXT.md: margin content snaps to body baselines, never to
     container tops.

     THE TRACKED UPPERCASE LINE MUST NOT WRAP. A tracked uppercase
     label that wraps is not a label — the finding that moved R4's
     margin from two columns to three.

     IT SETS AT A FIXED 10.5px. (R6.1.) R6 derived the size from the
     window with a container query, because its 42-character line did
     not fit a 3-column window at the register: it rendered 8.93px at
     1440 and 6.82px at 1146, which is not a caption. The expression
     was right and the copy was wrong. The line is now 26 characters
     and fits at 10.5px at every width the spread runs, so the
     container query, the cqw term and the min() are DELETED rather
     than kept as a fallback. Left in, they would silently shrink the
     type again the next time the copy changed, which is the failure
     this correction exists to remove — the same reason R4.1 deleted
     the sampled scrim colour instead of retaining it as an
     alternative.

     MEASURED, NOT CALCULATED. R6 shipped a 5% error from a
     per-character estimate and wrapped this line at 1440. The ink
     width per width is measured with a nowrap probe in the rendered
     page, at the rendered size, with the trailing letter-space
     removed, and it is in the R6.1 report. */
  .ath-caption-line{
    /* THE TRACKING IS DECLARED HERE AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT.
       .plate-caption sets 0.22em on the <p>, where it resolves once
       against the paragraph's font-size and INHERITS AS AN ABSOLUTE
       LENGTH. Declaring it in `em` on the element that actually
       carries the type makes it resolve against that element's own
       size. It is kept from R6 even though the two sizes now agree,
       because a tracking value that is only right by coincidence is a
       landmine for whichever pass changes one of the two. */
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
  }

  /* ---- THE DETAIL AND THE OVERLAP --------------------------------

     THE DEPTH IS DERIVED FROM THE TYPE, NOT SET IN PIXELS.

         --ath-lap = 4 x the body's line-height = 122.4px

     and the threshold it has to clear is measured, not estimated.
     CONTEXT.md's overlap floor is that an overlap is unambiguous or
     it is absent: the Cover's 54px version read as a text-overflow
     bug and was removed. The measurable form of "unambiguous" used
     here is THE OVERLAP MUST BE AT LEAST TWICE THE SECTION'S LARGEST
     INTERNAL VERTICAL GAP, because anything at or below that size can
     be read as a spacing accident rather than as a composition. This
     section's largest internal gap is body -> closing line, two body
     lines, 61.2px. Twice it is 122.4px, and --ath-lap is exactly
     that.

     Both terms come off --bio-size and --bio-lh, which are fixed at
     every band by design, so the lap is 122.4px at every width and
     the threshold moves with it if either token ever changes. The
     depth ACTUALLY ACHIEVED at each band is a different question —
     it depends on the frame clearing the type column — and it is
     measured per band in the report.

     GRID PLACEMENT AND A NEGATIVE MARGIN, never position:absolute
     with a hard offset. The detail is a grid item in row 5, whose top
     edge is the frame's bottom edge; a negative top margin of
     --ath-lap lifts it that far onto the frame. It survives a resize
     because it is expressed against a row boundary that moves with
     the layout.

     PAINT ORDER IS DOCUMENT ORDER. Both frames are position:relative
     via .plate-img and neither carries a z-index, so the later
     sibling paints over the earlier one. The detail is authored after
     the frame; nothing else is needed and no stacking context is
     invented. */
  .ath{
    --ath-lap: calc(4 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .ath-detail{
    margin: 0;
    margin-top: calc(var(--ath-lap) * -1);
  }

  /* THE DETAIL'S CROP.

     athletics-detail.jpg is 2633x3942 with no EXIF orientation, so
     the buffer and the presentation agree: native 0.66794.

     0.70 AGAINST THE BLEED FRAME'S 0.95. CONTEXT.md: aspect ratio is
     a separate lever from width and no two photographs on the page
     share a crop. These two touch each other, so the difference has
     to be legible rather than nominal — a 0.25 spread reads as two
     shapes, and 0.05 would read as one shape rendered twice.

     THE MECHANISM IS AN OVERSIZED BOX, NOT A TRANSFORM, because this
     crop is horizontal as well as vertical. Measured in R1: a static
     transform: scale() on the <img> takes the treated backdrop back
     to the raw source colour, because the transformed image
     composites into its own layer and .plate-img::before can no
     longer blend against it. So: a width greater than 100% with a
     matching negative left, both percentages, clipped by
     .plate-img's overflow: hidden.

     The wanted source window is x [0.14, 0.86], y [0.180, 0.867]:

         --crop-w   1 / 0.72              = 138.89%
         --crop-l   -(0.14 x 138.89)      = -19.44%
         box aspect  0.70 x 1.38889       =   0.9722
         visible y   0.66794 / 0.9722     =   0.687
         --crop-y    0.180 / (1 - 0.687)  =  57.5%

     The box aspect stays comfortably wider than the file's native
     0.66794, so object-fit spreads the full source width across the
     box and the horizontal identity holds. Both horizontal terms are
     percentages of the detail's OWN width, so its width cancels out
     of the source window entirely and the crop is identical at every
     viewport width in every band.

     What the window holds: the goal net at the top, both legs from
     mid-calf, the gold heel resting on the football at 51-68% of the
     frame's height, and turf below it. It cuts the goalposts, which
     is what makes it a detail rather than a second establishing
     shot, and it carries NO face — CONTEXT.md prefers detail crops
     with no face over another portrait, and this is the strongest
     single image in the set. */
  .ath-detail-frame{
    aspect-ratio: 0.70;
    --crop-w: 138.89%;
    --crop-l: -19.44%;
    --crop-y: 57.5%;
  }

  /* max-width: none is not optional. Tailwind's preflight sets
     `img { max-width: 100% }`, which clamps the oversized box back to
     the frame width and produces no horizontal crop at all. */
  .ath-detail-frame :global(img){
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--crop-l);
    width: var(--crop-w);
    max-width: none;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% var(--crop-y);
  }

  /* ---- THE STACK · <= 1145 ---------------------------------------
     ATHLETIC'S OWN CROSSOVER, measured and swept at 1px. The rule,
     the sweep and the reasoning are in global.css; it fires where the
     frame's rendered aspect falls below the source file's own
     presented aspect — the width at which the spread stops trimming
     sky off the photograph and starts cutting its sides.

     ONE STACKED ARRANGEMENT FROM 1145 ALL THE WAY DOWN, with only the
     column windows stepping by band. There is no separate phone
     treatment: the primitives already narrow the measure at 1023 and
     again at 900 and collapse it at 750, and everything a span cannot
     express is the same in all of them.

     The row track list comes off. There are no longer four rows for
     the frame to span, so `auto auto auto 1fr auto` would hand row 4
     a flex factor for nothing and open dead ground under the closing
     line. */
  @media (max-width: 1145px){
    .ath{ grid-template-rows: none; }

    /* THE LEFT BLEED SURVIVES THE STACK. The frame is above the type
       here rather than beside it, but it still runs off the left page
       trim, and that is the section — the broken gutter is what makes
       this Athletic Management rather than a photograph with a
       caption. Only band 4 goes to both trims, where a single column
       has no spread left to break. The inline-start margin is
       unchanged from the spread; what changes is the span, which the
       primitives handle. */
    .ath-bleed{
      /* The frame is no longer beside the type, so it needs a gap
         under it. Two body lines, from the body's own tokens — the
         same value and the same reasoning R4 uses in its own stacked
         bands. */
      margin-bottom: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    /* NOTHING NEEDS TO CHANGE ABOUT THE FRAME'S HEIGHT HERE, and that
       is a consequence of putting the ratio on the figure. Stacked,
       the figure is alone in its row, so the row is sized by the
       figure's own ratio-derived height and `height: 100%` on the
       frame resolves against it exactly as it does in the spread.
       R4.2's plate needs a stacked override because its ratio sits on
       the inner element; this one does not. */

    /* THE OVERLAP IS DROPPED, NOT SHRUNK. There is nothing left for
       it to lap onto — the frame runs to both trims, so an inset over
       it is a collage rather than a spread, and the two frames have
       no shared right edge to interlock on any more. CONTEXT.md's
       floor is explicit for exactly this case: an overlap is
       unambiguous or it is absent, and the Cover's 54px version is
       the precedent for removing rather than shipping it.

       62% of the measure, gutter-aligned — the same left edge every
       other block in the stack sits on, so the column reads as one
       measure with a photograph inset into it rather than as two
       photographs of different widths. The gap above it is the body's
       two lines again, matching the frame's. */
    .ath-detail{
      width: 62%;
      margin-top: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    /* THE CAPTION FALLS INLINE IN READING ORDER and takes ordinary
       rhythm rather than the baseline snap — a 28px lattice
       correction against a full-measure column reads as a stray line
       rather than as marginalia. Same treatment and same reasoning as
       Educational Leader's district link and Premier's topics list.

       IT NEEDS NO SIZE OVERRIDE. The line is a fixed 10.5px at every
       width on the page, and the stacked measure is wider than the
       spread's 3-column window at every width in this band, so if it
       fits in the spread it fits here. */
    .ath-caption{
      margin-top: var(--s4);
    }

    /* THE BOTTOM ANCHOR IS RELEASED WITH THE SPREAD. Stacked, the
       detail sits BELOW the closing line rather than beside it, so
       there is no image edge at the line's level to align to — the
       same reason About's snap gives way to ordinary rhythm in its
       own stacked bands. The primitive releases the span and the
       alignment; this releases the descender lift that went with
       them. The gap above the line is the body's derived two lines,
       unchanged from R6. */
    .ath-close{
      align-self: auto;
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---- BAND 4 · <= 750 -------------------------------------------
     THE FRAME GOES TO BOTH TRIMS, and only here. A one-sided bleed on
     a single-column page has no facing page and no gutter to break
     away from, so it would read as a misaligned photograph rather
     than as a composition. Both trims, or neither.

     Written against --gutter so it follows the token's phone clamp
     with nothing to re-measure. `body` already carries
     overflow-x: hidden, so it cannot open a horizontal scrollbar.

     THE FRAME'S ASPECT DOES NOT CHANGE HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
     At full bleed a 0.95 box is 395px tall on a 375px phone and 789px
     on a 750px tablet — taller than it is wide, which is the point.
     The obvious way to shorten it is to crop it wider, and that is
     exactly what CONTEXT.md refuses at spec time rather than solving
     by cropping: all client photography is vertical and a wide crop
     of a vertical photograph is not available at any band. The frame
     stays 0.95 and the section is simply tall on a phone, as every
     section is. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .ath-bleed{ margin-inline: calc(var(--gutter) * -1); }
  }
</style>
